<Base title="AC to DC Calculator: Rectifiers, Ripple & Regulation" titleIsComplete description="Calculate diode-drop rectifier averages, current or resistance-load capacitor ripple, and linear or supplied-efficiency regulator power with stated assumptions."><div class="shell page-intro"><Breadcrumbs items={[{label:'Home',href:'/'},{label:'AC to DC calculator'}]}/><p class="eyebrow">THE MODEL COMES FIRST</p><h1>AC to DC calculator</h1><p>Separate an unfiltered rectifier, a capacitor-filtered supply and a downstream DC regulator. Input conventions and assumptions change with the circuit.</p><ConverterWorkbench/><section class="prose education-section" id="methodology"><h2 id="formula">Calculation methodology</h2><h3>A · Basic constant-drop rectifier</h3><p>For sinusoidal input, Vpk = √2 Vrms. Let D = nVf, where n = 2 for a bridge and n = 1 for half-wave or a center-tapped path. Above threshold, θ0 = asin(D/Vpk). Integrating the clipped sine over a full cycle gives Vavg = [2 Vpk cos θ0 − D(π − 2θ0)] / π for full-wave, or half this value for half-wave. If D ≥ Vpk the output is zero.</p><p>This accounts for the non-conducting interval in a fixed-drop model. It does not subtract the diode drop from an ideal average indiscriminately. The output RMS is calculated from the integral of the squared clipped waveform. The separate <a href="/full-wave-rectifier-calculator/">ideal full-wave</a> and <a href="/half-wave-rectifier-calculator/">ideal half-wave tools</a> retain their zero-drop scope.</p><h3>B · Filtered current and resistance loads</h3><p>Recharge frequency fr is f for half-wave and 2f for single-phase full-wave. For constant current, ΔVpp = I/(fr C) and Vavg ≈ Vtop − ΔVpp/2. For resistance, x = 1/(fr R C); instantaneous crest recharging followed by exponential discharge gives Vvalley = Vtop exp(−x) and Vavg = Vtop [1 − exp(−x)]/x. Average load current is Vavg/R.</p><p>Both deliberately omit source resistance, finite charging time, capacitor ESR, leakage and temperature. A peak-to-peak ripple limit of 20% of the available crest is a scope guardrail, not a guaranteed error bound. Input capacitance is µF; the engine converts to farads. Center-tapped RMS is per winding half, not end-to-end.</p><h3>C · Conditional regulator-stage result</h3><p>Use the minimum raw DC input, including upstream ripple valley, and a device-specific headroom requirement. If that minimum is too low, regulation is rejected. Linear/LDO power balance includes quiescent current: Ploss = (Vin − Vout)Iout + Vin Iq. For the switching stage, efficiency is supplied by you; Pin = Pout/η. Neither mode predicts current capability, stability or junction temperature.</p><p>Efficiency is undefined when input power is zero. Regulator-stage efficiency does not include upstream AC input, transformer, rectifier or filter losses. Output switching ripple is not modeled.</p><h3>Checked numerical examples</h3><ul><li>12 V RMS bridge, 0.7 V per diode, 50 Hz, 0.1 A and 1,000 µF: crest 15.5706 V, ripple 1 V p-p, average 15.0706 V, valley 14.5706 V.</li><li>Zero-drop ideal full-wave at 12 V RMS: average 10.8038 V. Half-wave average: 5.4019 V.</li><li>Linear stage: minimum input 12 V, output 5 V at 1 A, Iq 1 mA: output 5 W, input 12.012 W, loss 7.012 W, stage efficiency about 41.625%.</li></ul><h3>References and independent checks</h3><p>Derivations are checked against independent numerical integration and boundary tests. See <a href="https://www.hammfg.com/electronics/transformers/rectifier">Hammond's topology/filter guide</a>, <a href="https://www.ti.com/lit/ug/tidub95/tidub95.pdf">TI TIDUB95 section 3.1 for capacitor charge and ripple</a>, and <a href="https://www.ti.com/lit/ml/slup415/slup415.pdf">TI's linear-regulator seminar for headroom, loss and efficiency context</a>. These are model checks, not physical measurements.</p><p>For broader context, explore <a href="/#circuit-diagrams">original circuit diagrams</a>, <a href="/blog/ac-to-dc-formulas/">formula selection</a>, and <a href="/blog/limits-of-simple-ac-to-dc-calculators/">the limits of a simple calculator</a>.</p></section></div></Base>
